# hc-patient-app — what Prettier does not format
#
# Two jobs. The first is the ordinary one: keep generated and vendored files out of scope. The
# second is the deliberate exclusion of `*.html`, which is a decision and not an oversight — read
# the second block before "fixing" it.
#
# This file exists so that a bare `npx prettier --check .` agrees with `npm run prettier:check`.
# Before it, the two disagreed: the npm scripts glob narrowly enough to miss `android/**/build/**`,
# thousands of generated Gradle files that the bare form walks, so they passed by luck rather than
# by design.
#
# An earlier draft of this paragraph also blamed the merged i18n bundles. That was wrong and is
# worth correcting rather than deleting: `src/assets/i18n/*.json` and `src/environments/i18n-hash.ts`
# were always inside the npm glob. They pass because `JSON.stringify(sorted, null, 2)` is already
# Prettier's output shape, not because anything missed them. They are listed below as generated
# output, which is a different and better reason.

# --- deliberate: Angular templates are hand-formatted ------------------------
#
# NO PRETTIER VERSION INDENTS INSIDE ANGULAR CONTROL FLOW. Measured 2026-09-08 against every
# release from 2.8.8 to 4.0.0-alpha.13: all of them flatten the contents of an `@if`/`@for` block
# to the parent's level, so
#
#     @if (item.notes) {                 becomes    @if (item.notes) {
#       <div class="hc-prose">…</div>               <div class="hc-prose">…</div>
#     }                                             }
#
# and a nested block loses its structure entirely. The 12 templates here are indented by hand and
# that is the readable form, so Prettier is pointed away from them rather than allowed to degrade
# them. This is not a stale pin waiting to be bumped: there is no newer version that fixes it, and
# raising the pin changes nothing about this file.
#
# `src/index.html` has no control flow and does conform — it is excluded with the rest so there is
# one rule about HTML here rather than two.
#
# BE CLEAR ABOUT WHAT THIS COSTS: templates are now checked by nothing but the AOT compiler in
# `build:prod`. An earlier draft of this comment claimed `npm run lint` still gated their
# correctness through @angular-eslint/eslint-plugin-template — IT DOES NOT. That plugin and
# @angular-eslint/template-parser are both in devDependencies and both wired to nothing: the plugin
# is not in `plugins`, the template parser is configured nowhere, and `lint` runs
# `eslint . --ext .js,.mjs,.ts`, which never opens a `.html` file at all. So a11y rules,
# banana-in-box, and template `eqeqeq` run nowhere. That gap predates this file and is backlog
# item 21; it is written down here because this is where somebody will come looking for the
# reassurance the earlier draft wrongly gave them.
*.html

